WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.240
The Austro-Hungarian navy is often times overlooked
in history as a meager attempt of trying to

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.579
catch up to the rest of the European powers.

00:00:25.579 --> 00:00:30.050
However this view isn’t entirely correct,
as the Imperial navy was far from small, and

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.000
at one point was the 7th largest navy in the
world.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.440
Plus the technology used on its ships, like
radios was far from outdated and the naval

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.620
structure based of off the british navy was
on par with every other super power of the

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:42.960
early 20th century.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.331
So with all this said, why people today don’t
know much about the Austro-Hungarian navy

00:00:46.331 --> 00:00:49.300
let alone know that Austria-Hungary even had
a navy.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:53.059
This is mostly due to the fact that even though
the Austro-Hungarian navy was strong in its

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:56.840
own right it was always treated as an afterthought
in the Empire.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.980
This can be seen through the fact that the
navy was part of the war ministry which was

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.610
controlled by the military, as such the naval
budget was very small numbering only around

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:10.420
18 million crowns in 1899, this was 5 times
less compared to the Italian naval budget

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:14.210
and 30 and a half times less compared to the
British naval budget.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:18.170
However this naval budget would slowly start
to increase going in to World War 1, not only

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.860
because of the looming threat of war but also
due to the fact that Archduke Franz Ferdinand

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.690
was very keen on having a large and strong
navy as he saw the benefits such naval power

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.240
can bring to the country.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.630
He heavily campaigned on the side of the navy
and ironically it could be said that without

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.000
him the Imperial navy wouldn’t even be ready
for world war 1.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.640
But the lack of money was far from the only
problem the Austro-Hungarian navy had to face.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.860
For one the multicultural nature of the empire
meant that you could hear 8 different languages

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.590
or more spoken on any given ship and officers
were required to know at least 4 of these

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.590
languages.

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:55.110
Naturally such structure didn’t always result
in the most smoothest of order executions.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:59.250
When it came to the sailors them self, majority
of them in the 19th century came from Croatia

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:04.200
and Italy as they were largely the only ethnicities
in the empire that had any extensive sailing

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.200
experience.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.119
However this started to change in the early
20th century when the involvement of minorities

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.220
became more prominent across the empire and
the naval recruitment and training became

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.420
more standardized.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:18.120
With this increased recruitment of different
ethnic groups, to avoid any confusions on

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.560
a ship, people of the same/similar ethnicities
where often times given similar roles so they

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.680
could communicate orders more easily.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:26.680
I.E.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.370
Germans (Austrian), Czechs, and Slovaks where
gunners and machinists, Hungarians where anti-air

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.970
and machine-gun crew, Croats and Slovenes
where deckhands and stokers, etc.

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:39.420
Lastly, probably the most major problem facing
the Austro-Hungarian navy, was in the construction

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.420
of it’s ships.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.310
At first the construction was limited due
to the lack of Hungarian parliamentary support

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:47.750
who viewed the navy as a pointless expensive
waist of money.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:51.450
However as an all out war started be more
and more inevitable they backed down with

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:52.569
their opposition.

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:56.370
But with that another major problem started
to come to light, which was the fact that

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.370
the double nature of the monarchy meant that
all governmental industrial spending had to

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:07.420
be divided between the Hungarian part and
the Austrian part, 34.4% to 65.6% respectively.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:13.470
With that said the 34.4% still proved problematic
for the navy to spend in hungary as majority

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:17.130
of the Austro-Hungarian industry was in the
Austrian half of the empire.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:21.710
And whatever little industry was in the Hungarian
part wasn’t up to date to be able to build

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:24.770
the high tech ships that the Imperial navy
had designed.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:29.150
So the ship designs had to be altered for
the Hungarian industry to be able to build

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.650
the 34.4% of the ships.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.790
Most notable of these changes was the use
of less powered engines, different electrical

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.980
wiring, different boilers, the use of different
screws and bolts, etc.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.599
These ships would later on prove not as reliable
as the ones build in the Austrian part of

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:48.020
the empire, however what was remarkable was
that even due to all these changes the ships

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.740
build in the Hungarian part weren’t far
behind in benchmarks compared to the Austrian

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.740
ships.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.310
With all that said the limited government
funding and the division of construction between

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.440
the two parts of the empire resulted in the
fact that the Austro-Hungarian navy had to

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.100
very heavily budget its operations to remain
relevant on the world stage.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:10.990
Such budgeting included restricted use of
high class ships, lower number of active operational

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:15.959
squadrents, building ships with a lower overall
tonnage and mostly inclusive lower wages compared

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:16.959
to its rivals.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:21.560
However this budgeting proved to be quite
effective as at the break of World War 1 Austria-Hungary

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.690
had 110 ships and 5 submarines with 3 extra
dreadnoughts in construction, in comparison

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:33.460
the italian navy which was viewed as the main
rival of the Imperial navy, had only 149 ships

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.430
with 21 submarines and no boats in active
construction.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:40.550
And even though it may be more compared to
what the Imperial navy had, considering how

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:45.199
much of a larger budget the Italian navy had
to work with, it wasn’t that big of a difference.

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:50.080
Therefor going into world war one the Austro-Hungarian
navy was far from being a pushover and the

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.259
Allies had to seriously consider the Imperial
navy’s strength especially when it came

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:56.699
to planning mediterranean operations.

00:04:56.699 --> 00:05:01.159
Speaking of which, when it came to the preparations
for WW1 the Imperial navy had three battle

00:05:01.159 --> 00:05:02.370
strategies in mind.

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:06.419
The first was conceived prior to the breakup
of the triple alliance when Italy was still

00:05:06.419 --> 00:05:08.199
considered a viable ally.

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:12.139
This plan involved the Austro-Hungarian navy
and the Italian navy to use Italian bases

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:16.699
in Sicily to block the French but mainly the
British from crossing the mediterranean and

00:05:16.699 --> 00:05:18.690
consequently the suez canal.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:22.300
This would mean that Britain would be cut
off from its fast rout in to the Eastern colonies

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:26.300
and would have to use the long way around
which would drastically diminish it’s fighting

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.240
power especially when it came to a drawn out
war.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:33.659
However this strategy was far from fool prove
as both the Italian and Imperial navies had

00:05:33.659 --> 00:05:37.629
their doubts about being able to keep the
French and British navies from crossing the

00:05:37.629 --> 00:05:38.729
mediterranean.

00:05:38.729 --> 00:05:43.110
As such the back up plan was to use the bases
in Sicily to heavily haras allied shipping

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:47.809
in the area so that it proved too costly for
the Allies to use those shipping routes.

00:05:47.809 --> 00:05:52.190
However this entire naval plan fell through
when it became increasingly clear that Italy

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:55.479
wasn’t going to follow through on its promise
to the triple alliance.

00:05:55.479 --> 00:05:59.939
As such the second plan of the Austro-Hungarian
navy was to do a preemptive strike on the

00:05:59.939 --> 00:06:02.830
main Italian ports in the Adriatic.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:06.620
Effectively cripeling the Italian ability
to do naval operations in the area and anabeling

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:10.370
of the Imperial navy to take full control
of that part of the mediterranean.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.939
Third and also the most conservative plan
of the Austro-Hungarian navy was to use its

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:18.639
vast number of torpedo boats to focus on the
defence of their Adriatic coast line, protecting

00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:22.979
valuable ports, towns and shipping, all while
threatening to attack with the rest of its

00:06:22.979 --> 00:06:24.530
navy at any moment.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:29.369
This way they could keep the much larger french
and italian mediterranean navis in check effectively

00:06:29.369 --> 00:06:32.129
hindering their actions in other theaters
of the war.

00:06:32.129 --> 00:06:36.879
When the great war started in 1914 the initial
neutrality of Italy meant that the Imperial

00:06:36.879 --> 00:06:41.490
navy had to implement their defencive tactic
as they couldn't execute their preemptive

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:44.309
bombardment of the Italian adriatic coastline.

00:06:44.309 --> 00:06:48.819
When Italy finally joined the war in 1915
the imperial navy executed their second plan

00:06:48.819 --> 00:06:54.990
however this proved ineffective as their “preemptive”
strike proved not so preemptive as Italy had

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.999
ample time to prepare for it.

00:06:56.999 --> 00:07:00.979
With that the Imperial navy went back to their
defencive strategy, which proved effective,

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:04.629
because the Allied navies didn’t want to
risk a full on naval battle with them, resorting

00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:08.369
to small battle missions and the blockade
of the Otranto Straits.

00:07:08.369 --> 00:07:12.569
This meant that there were continues small
skirmishes/battles throughout the adriatic,

00:07:12.569 --> 00:07:16.589
like the battle of the Otranto straits where
the Imperial navy manage to score a victory,

00:07:16.589 --> 00:07:21.599
but that battle just as every other Imperial
naval battle didn’t result any decisive

00:07:21.599 --> 00:07:22.599
changes.

00:07:22.599 --> 00:07:26.819
So the Austro Hungarian navy excluding its
submarines was forced to operate only in the

00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:27.839
adriatic.

00:07:27.839 --> 00:07:31.809
This stagnation combined with a dwindling
coal supply, meant that the Austro-Hungarian

00:07:31.809 --> 00:07:35.110
navy didn’t go out with a bang but with
fizzle.

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.949
Keeping the allied armies at bay in the adriatic
but remaining largely stagnant till the end

00:07:39.949 --> 00:07:44.559
of the war, after which it got divided between
the Allies and the newly formed Yugoslavia

00:07:44.559 --> 00:07:47.249
and then mostly disassembled for scrap.

00:07:47.249 --> 00:07:51.360
And with that the Austro-Hungarian navy which
was able to hold its own against the allied

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.099
navies got mostly forgotten.

00:07:54.099 --> 00:07:58.699
Remaining today largely as a small footnote
when talking about the Austro-Hungarian Empire.

